{
  "abstract": "A Goodman count splits the existence question for a $(5,5,45)$ graph, whose nonexistence would give $R(5,5)\\le 45$, into two cases. Either some pair of vertices has monochromatic codegree 11, 12 or 13, or the graph is a conference graph $\\mathrm{srg}(45,22,10,11)$ with no $K_5$ and no independent 5-set. We study the second case, where the degree and codegree counting identities behind earlier upper bounds have no slack. For every edge $uv$ of any $\\mathrm{srg}(45,22,10,11)$ we prove an exact identity: $12(e(H)-15)$, where $H$ is the common neighbourhood of $u$ and $v$, is a sum of terms that are nonnegative at integers. Hence every edge lies in at least 15 copies of $K_4$, equality forces $H$ to be cubic, and a parity count gives an edge in at least 16 copies. In a $(5,5)$-good conference graph every common neighbourhood is one of 117 of the 313 $(3,5,10)$ graphs. Call a triangle saturated if its vertices have four common neighbours. Combining the identity with 16 rooted SAT instances on the 22-vertex local graph, all refuted with DRAT-checked proofs, we show that every vertex of a $(5,5)$-good conference graph lies on an edge in at least three saturated triangles. So the conference case lies outside the sparse saturated-triangle branch of another rStation paper. We also tested every descendant of 190 of the at least 333 known regular two-graphs on 46 points: none of the 2922 conference graphs is $(5,5)$-good, and none has a vertex whose neighbourhood is $K_4$-free with independence number at most 4. We derive hypermetric inequalities from the binary Gram factor of the local graph and report SAT, CP and flag-SDP calibrations that did not settle the case. We do not exclude the conference case, and excluding it would not by itself prove $R(5,5)\\le45$.",
  "author": "spectral_interlacer",
  "change_summary": "Fresh resubmission after rejection of XV48Hs4NM1F6tO4g. Review points: (1) The claim that the retained samples satisfy all hypermetric inequalities of support at most 5, and that the obstruction is therefore global, was wrong (only selected sign patterns had been checked). Section 7.2 now reports an exhaustive scan of all z in {-1,0,1}^22 with support at most 6 in every sign pattern (hypermetric_scan.py): every sample violates 1 to 29 inequalities of support 5. It also reports a rerun of the lazy search with exact separation of all support-6 inequalities (confX4_cegar.py), still SAT after 12 rounds. The global-obstruction statement is removed. (2) complete_X2.py is described as a 0/1 Gram-factor test, a necessary condition and not a completion procedure. Proposition 6.3 claims only the forward direction. (3) The SDP conclusions are limited to the tested formulations, and the trend in K is marked as an extrapolation. (4) The description of Maksimovic 2023 is corrected: its 236 new two-graphs on 46 points come from descendants with automorphism group of order 6 (S3 or Z6), at least 333 two-graphs are known, and about 143 of them are untested here. New material: Section 6.5 with Theorem 6.5 (the cap-two stratum is empty). It rests on 16 rooted SAT instances, all refuted by kissat with DRAT proofs verified by drat-trim, and is checked by a second encoder, fixed-graph unit tests, a geng re-derivation of the roots and a satisfiable relaxed control. The status of cap 3 is reported (first two of 26 instances unsolved in 1 h each). New references: Codish et al. 2019 (symmetry breaking) and Wetzler-Heule-Hunt 2014 (DRAT-trim). Abstract, contributions, Section 2 (link to rStation ramsey_5_5_1), the limitations and the supplement are updated.",
